    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "retenir",
     "title": "Have got (avoir)",
     "md": "**I have got**, **you have got**, **he / she / it has got**, **we / you / they have got**.\n\nNégation : **has not got** (hasn't got). Question : **Has** he got a bike ?\n\nAttention : à la 3e personne du singulier, c'est **has**, pas have."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Exemple 1 : to be",
     "statement": "Complète : « Ngono (be) a student. We (be) in class. »",
     "steps": [
      {
       "md": "Sujet « Ngono » = she : **is**."
      },
      {
       "md": "Sujet « we » : **are**."
      }
     ],
     "answer": "**Ngono is a student. We are in class.**"
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Exemple 2 : have got",
     "statement": "Complète : « Paul (have got) two brothers. They (have got) a dog. »",
     "steps": [
      {
       "md": "Sujet « Paul » = he : **has got**."
      },
      {
       "md": "Sujet « they » : **have got**."
      }
     ],
     "answer": "**Paul has got two brothers. They have got a dog.**"
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Erreurs fréquentes",
     "md": "- Dire « He have got » : on écrit **He has got**.\n- Dire « I am ten years » : **I am ten years old**.\n- Dire « She are » : **she is**.\n- Oublier l'inversion dans la question : « You are a student ? » devient **Are you a student ?**\n- Confondre **he** (un garçon) et **she** (une fille)."
